{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795341116,"nonce":"Tl8k9g13Kpln/qJNiW2TNdujOyo=","message":"Fixed in 73c6eb377492fc8afea01ab1b944dbfe197a888a (local, not pushed).\n\nCause, from a trace of how the function was entered: its first call came from an interpreted frame function, and that frame function was compiled during the long first call. Every later call was compiled to compiled and ticked nothing, so the promote postponed by the OSR hand-off was never asked for again. My first reading, that the first entry came through the stub bridge, was wrong for this program; that is a second shape, and it is covered too.\n\nThe ledger from 146013d now counts two more events as a re-entry: compiled code entering the function through the stub bridge (the first entry only), and the hand-off frame returning into a function that is compiled. A promote already postponed is queued; one not yet decided is not postponed. A function whose caller is still interpreted when its frame returns, such as a loop owner called once from main, is still postponed.\n\nBoth hooks are needed. With the bridge hook disabled, the variant where the caller is compiled before the first call defers again; the other variant still passes through the return hook. Tests: two new cases in crates/ash_cli/tests/handoff_reentry.rs, one per variant (fixture TestCompiledCaller.hx). Both fail on the previous binary with no LLVM install and pass now. Ledger unit tests, osr_retier, nested_strip and tree_fusion pass.\n\nReproducer (CCBench3, 30 calls of a 4096 x 1000 Single loop from a compiled frame function), ns per element, NUC pinned to cores 0,2,4,6: previous binary 2.2 on every call; now 2.2 until the LLVM install lands, then 1.3. Mac: 3.5 on every call before, 1.34 after.\n\nNUC A/B, 13 benchmarks, hybrid, interleaved against 5c04780b: nothing outside noise. Largest moves are fib +2.7% (a 12 ms run with 5.8% spread) and stdlib -1.4%. method_call -0.1%, closure_call +0.9%.\n\nNot covered: a caller that compiles after the hand-off frame has returned and before the function's second call. Not reproduced.","files":null}]}